{"byteLength":618,"contentHash":{"algorithm":"sha256","value":"562a3bc79b0314ab98f4ed3911494478134d180938e067fba84b4b6ffe6b94d4"},"kind":"skillDocument","name":"references/stdlib/Triple.md","producer":{"build":{"commit":"5de2594ad523fba4f38fa63f7a1b306e8a23eb0c","dirty":false,"provenance":{"commit":"git","dirty":"environment"},"target":"aarch64-apple-darwin"},"name":"benitoite","version":"0.0.13"},"schemaVersion":1,"text":"# Benitoite.Triple\n\nStatus: standard, in the prelude. It can be used without `import`.\n\nRefer to its declarations as `Triple.<name>`, for example `Triple.Triple`.\n\nTriples of three values.\n\n```benitoite\npublic data Triple[A, B, C]\n  Triple(A, B, C)\nend data\n```\n\nA triple of three values, written `Triple(a, b, c)`.\n\n```benitoite\npublic function first[A, B, C](t: Triple[A, B, C]) -> A\n```\n\nReturns the first value.\n\n```benitoite\npublic function second[A, B, C](t: Triple[A, B, C]) -> B\n```\n\nReturns the second value.\n\n```benitoite\npublic function third[A, B, C](t: Triple[A, B, C]) -> C\n```\n\nReturns the third value.\n"}
